Durarara!! AU
Chapter 12 – Part 1
Curtis set his chopsticks down and looked around the table, expression calm but unimpressed by the heavier framing.
“You all make the Dollars sound like some gang you can’t leave.” He gave a small shrug. “From my own knowledge, it’s just a bunch of bored internet kids. Some bored adults.”
The table went quiet for a beat.
Kadota studied him over the rim of his glass, the usual steady assessment in his eyes.
“…That’s the surface of it, yeah,” he said eventually. “No colors. No formal ranks. No official way to get jumped out. Most of the people in it are just bored. Kids on their phones. Adults with nothing better to do. They join, they lurk, they show up once in a while if something interesting is happening, and then they go back to their regular lives.”
Walker nodded, though his expression had turned more serious.
“The problem isn’t the bored ones. It’s that the Dollars got big enough that the people who aren’t bored started using the same name. Real predators. Old color-gang leftovers. Information brokers. People who like the anonymity because it lets them do ugly things without a banner over their heads.”
Erika leaned her chin on one hand, watching Curtis.
“So you’re not wrong. For most members it’s basically a big, chaotic group chat with occasional real-world meetups. But once something bad happens under the Dollars name, it sticks to everyone who carries the label—whether they were involved or not. That’s the part that makes it dangerous for someone like Mikado.”
Saburo grunted in agreement.
“Kid thinks it’s an adventure. He doesn’t understand that the bored internet majority and the people who use the Dollars as cover for real work are wearing the same invisible badge.”
Masaomi stayed quiet through the explanations, eyes on Curtis, clearly waiting to see how the transfer student would respond to the fuller picture.
Curtis set his elbows on the table and loosely laced his fingers together, mismatched eyes calm.
“Tell me about your colored gangs. I’ve heard the names Blue Squares and Yellow Scarves.”
The shift in the air was immediate.
Kadota’s posture straightened by a fraction. Walker’s usual animated expression flattened. Erika’s fingers stopped moving over her phone. Saburo’s jaw tightened. Across from Curtis, Masaomi went still in a way that was too controlled to be casual.
Curtis’s gaze moved deliberately—first to Masaomi, then to Kadota, then across the other three at the table. He took in the micro-reactions the same way he had taken in everything else since arriving in Ikebukuro: cleanly, without apology.
“I count four former Blue Squares,” he said evenly, “and one Yellow Scarf.”
A short pause. The quiet around the table had gone heavy.
“You four need to learn to hide your facial reactions and body language.”
No one spoke right away.
Kadota was the first to recover enough to answer, voice low and measured.
“…You’re guessing.”
It wasn’t a strong denial. It was a test.
Walker let out a short, disbelieving breath.
“He’s not guessing. Look at him. He’s just saying it.”
Erika had gone unusually quiet, studying Curtis with a different kind of interest now—less otaku delight, more wary assessment of someone who could read a table that cleanly.
Saburo muttered under his breath,
“This is why we don’t bring sharp strangers to sushi.”
Masaomi hadn’t looked away from Curtis. The earlier fragile ease from their walk had thinned again, replaced by the same cold calculation he wore whenever someone got too close to the old history.
“…You’re full of surprises, Nightstar,” he said finally, voice flat. “Most people need years in this city to start putting those names together. You’re doing it over lunch.”
His eyes narrowed slightly.
“How much do you actually know—and how much are you just fishing?”
Curtis reached into the inner pocket of his blue uniform jacket and pulled out a small, well-worn pocket book. The cover was creased soft from repeated handling. He set it on the table between the sushi plates without ceremony.
“This is my fourth book.” His voice stayed even. “I’ve been pursuing detective work after college. So my apologies if I’m able to deduce and figure out some small-time street gangs.”
The table stared at the little book like it was a loaded weapon.
Walker leaned in first, eyes wide.
“He has a physical study guide for reading people. That’s actually insane. In a cool way. Mostly cool.”
Erika picked up the book carefully, flipped a few pages, and let out a low whistle.
“Micro-expressions, baseline behavior, stress indicators… he’s been training for this. No wonder he clocked the Blue Squares and Yellow Scarves history in one conversation.”
Kadota exhaled slowly through his nose, some of the sharper tension leaving his shoulders—replaced by a more complicated respect.
“…So it’s deliberate,” he said. “Not just natural talent. You’re building the skill on purpose.”
Saburo shook his head, still looking unsettled.
“Great. A foreigner with a textbook on how to see through all of us. Perfect. Love that for the group.”
Masaomi’s gaze moved from the pocket book back to Curtis’s face. The comparison to Izaya didn’t vanish, but it shifted. Izaya collected people for entertainment and leverage. This looked more like someone methodically sharpening himself for a specific future.
“…Detective work,” Masaomi repeated, quieter than before. “You mentioned that on the walk. You’re serious about it.”
He leaned back slightly, the defensive edge still present but no longer purely hostile.
“Most people who can read a table this cleanly either end up as cops, criminals, or information brokers. You’re telling us you’re aiming for the first one.” A short pause. “Forgive us if we verify that the hard way.”
Curtis gave a small, humorless breath at the mention of information brokers.
“An info broker, huh.” He turned the pocket book over once in his fingers before sliding it back into his jacket. “If I ever do become one, it would be by taking everything from that rat Izaya and giving back what he stole from people over the years… if it’s possible.”
The table absorbed that in silence for a second. The open dislike for Izaya was something most of them could understand, even if the method sounded ambitious.
Curtis continued, mismatched eyes steady.
“Now—as for Mikado. Something’s been gnawing at him since he got to the city. I noticed it the day I got into it with Shizuo. His attitude began to dip after that.”
Masaomi’s expression tightened with reluctant recognition.
“…Yeah. I noticed the change too.” He leaned forward slightly, elbows on the table. “He’s been quieter in some ways and more restless in others. Keeps bringing up the Dollars, keeps looking for ways to do something. I thought it was just the usual small-town-kid-in-the-big-city itch. You’re saying it started around the same time you showed up hard enough to put Shizuo on the ground.”
Kadota watched the exchange without interrupting. Walker and Erika had gone quieter, filing the information away. Saburo just shook his head like the web of teenage problems was already too tangled.
None of them had the full picture.
Elsewhere, walking home alone with the image of Seiji and the unknown girl still sitting unresolved in his mind, Mikado Ryūgamine felt the same low, persistent irritation that had been following him since the first day of school. Every time Curtis Nightstar stepped in—class rep, the street fight, Nasujima in the hallway, the easy way Anri looked at him after a few honest sentences—it settled a little deeper.
He didn’t have a clean name for it yet.
But it was jealousy. And it was growing.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes moved across the table, calm and deliberate.
“Mikado showed up the same day I did. What changed after that?”
The question sat in the middle of the sushi plates for a second.
Then the timeline clicked into place for several people at once.
Kadota was the first to speak, voice slow as he worked it out.
“…The night of the street performance. Masked guy with the guitar. Hood drops, braids come out, whole crowd stops. That was the same night Mikado was first really out in the city with us.”
Walker’s eyes widened.
“No—he’s right. Mikado had just gotten here. We were walking, heard the music, stopped for the busker… and that busker was you.” He pointed at Curtis. “Gray hoodie. Black mask. Sunglasses. Jumpman cap. The whole covered-up look. Then the hood fell and suddenly there were braids and a completely different silhouette.”
Erika nodded, already pulling up the mental image.
“Full night outfit. Oversized gray hoodie with that little emblem, black face mask, dark lenses, black Jumpman hat. You looked like you were trying not to exist until the music started. Then you became impossible to ignore.”
Saburo leaned back in his chair.
“And ever since that night, Mikado’s been… off. Restless. Watching you. Comparing.”
Masaomi’s expression had gone tighter as the pieces lined up. The day Curtis Nightstar arrived in force—rooftop, street performance, concrete cracks, the whole entrance—was the same day Mikado’s quiet dissatisfaction started growing teeth.
Curtis simply listened, long black braids still against the blue uniform jacket, and let them finish connecting it themselves.
Curtis raised both hands slightly in a mild, defensive gesture.
“Hey now. I didn’t even know any of you. I can’t be the root cause…”
Even as he said it, the thought moved cleanly through his mind:
I gotta go. It’s getting late and there are some shops I want to check out. Mikado can wait.
He started to stand.
Masaomi’s head turned sharply.
“What?”
Curtis met his eyes without flinching, already collecting the last of his things.
“He’s not my best bud. And he’s not at the top of my priority list.” The words were calm, almost practical. “I’m here if you need my help. But I still got a life to live, Masaomi.”
He gave the table a short, polite nod—acknowledgement without apology—and turned toward the door of Russia Sushi.
Walker immediately half-rose out of his seat.
“Wait, wait, wait—you can’t just drop the Blue Squares / Yellow Scarves read, the detective books, the timeline connection and the Izaya theft fantasy and then leave. I have follow-up questions—”
Erika was already reaching out like she could physically keep him at the table with sheer enthusiasm.
“At least tell us which Chainsaw Man arc hit you hardest before you disappear again. And whether the braids are heat-trained or traditional. And—”
Curtis kept walking.
The bell above the Russia Sushi door gave its soft ring as he stepped back out into the late afternoon, long black braids shifting against the blue uniform jacket, already angling toward whatever shops he had decided mattered more than sitting with other people’s crises for the rest of the day.
Behind him, the table was left with unfinished sushi, a lingering tension, and two very frustrated otaku.
Curtis paused with one hand on the door, long black braids shifting as he glanced back over his shoulder at the table. His mismatched eyes moved between Erika and Walker with mild, genuine curiosity.
“Are you and Walker a couple? I’ve been wondering that, but can’t quite place my finger on it.”
The effect was instant.
Walker’s face went red so fast it looked like someone had flipped a switch. Erika made a short, high sound that was half denial and half short-circuit, her own face matching his within a second. Kadota closed his eyes like a man who had seen this exact dynamic too many times. Saburo choked on his drink. Masaomi just stared.
In the brief window of pure, sputtering chaos, Curtis slipped out.
The bell above the door rang softly. By the time Walker managed a strangled “W-we’re not—!” and Erika started tripping over her own rebuttal, the tall transfer student with the braids was already gone, the late afternoon street swallowing him as cleanly as if he had never sat down.
Inside Russia Sushi, the table was left with unfinished sushi, unanswered questions, two thoroughly flustered otaku, and the distinct feeling that Curtis Nightstar had just won an exit he hadn’t even been trying to win.
On his way out the door, a deep, cheerful voice followed him.
“Thank you, friend Curtis! Coming again!”
Simon raised one massive hand in a warm, uncomplicated wave from behind the counter, already treating the tall foreigner’s presence as a settled fact of the restaurant. Curtis gave a short nod over his shoulder in return and stepped fully into the street, the door swinging shut behind him.
Inside, the table was still recovering.
Walker had both hands on his face.
“He just—he just said it. Out loud. Like it was a normal question. Who does that?!”
Erika was still pink, alternating between furious typing on her phone and glaring at the door like she could telepathically drag Curtis back in to finish the conversation properly.
“He reads micro-expressions, clocks gang history, connects timelines, and drops relationship speculation as an exit line. I’m going to lose my mind. In a literary way.”
Kadota rubbed his temples.
“We’re never having a quiet meal again.”
Saburo just shook his head and reached for the remaining sushi like the only sane response was to keep eating.
Masaomi stayed quiet longer than the others.
He stared at the door Curtis had used, the earlier tension from the Blue Squares / Yellow Scarves read and the detective-book reveal still sitting under his ribs. The couple comment had been chaotic, but it wasn’t what stayed with him. What stayed was the ease with which Curtis had walked in, taken the measure of everyone at the table, offered limited help on his own terms, and then left the second the conversation no longer served him.
He’s not Izaya.
But he decides when he’s done with a room the same way.
Masaomi exhaled slowly and finally looked back at the others.
“…He’s going to be a problem,” he muttered. “I just can’t tell yet if he’s our problem or everyone else’s.”
Kadota had been watching Masaomi more than the door.
He set his glass down and spoke in the steady, practical tone he used when he was cutting through noise.
“You’re overthinking the threat level.” He glanced around the table once, then back to Masaomi. “Nightstar doesn’t feel like an enemy. Doesn’t feel like a friend either. He’s closer to Shizuo than anything else at this table.”
Walker blinked.
“Shizuo? The walking natural disaster?”
“In a way,” Kadota said. “Same kind of presence. Same sense that if something goes wrong, the street pays for it. Difference is, Nightstar’s got more patience. He sits. He listens. He reads the room. Shizuo usually hits first and sorts it out never.”
Masaomi let out a short, rough breath that was almost a laugh and almost not.
“…Yeah. Until he does get pissed.” He looked at Kadota, the memory of the sidewalk still clear. “You weren’t there for the full thing. Trash can. Board to the head. Two stab wounds from Izaya. And when he finally stopped being calm, he put Shizuo on the ground and nearly folded Izaya into a bike rack. The patience runs out—and when it does, he’s exactly like Shizuo. Same strength. Same total lack of interest in holding back.”
The table went quieter.
Erika’s earlier excitement had cooled into something more thoughtful. Walker looked torn between fascination and low-grade alarm. Saburo just muttered,
“So we’ve got a second Shizuo, only this one does homework on micro-expressions and quotes Chainsaw Man. Perfect.”
Kadota didn’t disagree. He simply nodded once, accepting the fuller picture Masaomi had just added, and reached for the last of the sushi like the only reasonable response was to keep eating while the city kept producing new problems.
It didn’t take long for the serious talk to collapse under Erika and Walker’s natural gravity.
Once Kadota and Masaomi had finished weighing whether Curtis was closer to Shizuo or something more complicated, the two otaku had already drifted sideways into a completely different question.
Walker leaned forward, still slightly pink from the earlier couple comment but recovering fast.
“Okay but real talk—does he cosplay?”
Erika’s eyes lit up immediately.
“I’ve been thinking about that since the street performance. The full covered look that first night? Gray hoodie, black mask, dark lenses, Jumpman cap—that wasn’t just ‘I don’t want to be recognized.’ That was a deliberate silhouette. Clean. Intentional. Like he put the outfit together knowing exactly how it would read under streetlights.”
Walker nodded hard.
“And then the next day he’s in the school uniform with the braids out and the heterochromia on full display like it’s nothing. Most people who go that hard on a masked look don’t switch into clean-cut so easily. That’s range. That’s someone who understands presentation.”
Erika tapped her phone against the table.
“We only have two confirmed versions so far. The night busker and the daytime student. But the way he carries both of them? I refuse to believe there isn’t at least one more look in that closet. Nobody with that sense of presence stops at two.”
Kadota gave them a flat look.
“You two are doing character analysis on a guy who just walked out of here after calling you a couple.”
“And he wasn’t even wrong to wonder,” Walker muttered, then immediately looked mortified that he’d said it out loud.
Masaomi rubbed his face.
“We’re not discussing whether Nightstar cosplays. We’re discussing the fact that he can put Shizuo on the ground and reads micro-expressions out of a pocket book for fun.”
Erika waved him off without looking away from her notes.
“We can do both. Multi-tasking. It’s called being a complex audience.”
Neither of them had any idea about the sharp gray-and-red jacket, the gold sunglasses, or the glowing-visored helmet. Those versions still belonged only to Rio—and, in one carefully smoke-hidden encounter, to Anri. For now, the van gang’s theories were built entirely on the masked street entrance and the calm classroom transfer student, and that was already more than enough to keep Walker and Erika occupied for the rest of the meal.
Back inside Russia Sushi, the conversation finally circled to the question Masaomi had been carrying since the sidewalk.
“Can I trust him?”
The table went quieter.
Erika answered first, still half in her notes.
“I think so. He didn’t have to help Anri. He didn’t have to sit here and answer anything. And he walked out instead of fishing for leverage. That counts for something.”
Walker nodded.
“Yeah. He’s weird and sharp and probably dangerous, but he doesn’t feel like the type who collects people just to break them. I’d trust him further than most strangers in this city.”
Kadota was more measured.
“Be careful. We still don’t know enough. He shows up, cracks concrete, puts Shizuo down, reads all of us like a book, and then leaves when he’s done talking. That kind of person can be an ally or a problem depending on the day. Don’t hand him anything you can’t afford to lose.”
Saburo grunted in agreement.
“What he said. Trust is earned slower than sushi.”
Simon chose that moment to appear at the side of the table with a fresh pot of tea, his massive frame casting a familiar shadow.
“Curtis is good person,” he said simply, in his warm, heavy accent. “Strong. Quiet. His Russian is good too.”
The table froze.
Walker’s chopsticks stopped halfway to his mouth.
“…His Russian?”
Erika stared.
“He speaks Russian? On top of Japanese? And whatever else?”
Masaomi’s expression tightened with a new layer of calculation.
Of course he does.
Detective books. Micro-expressions. Multiple languages. Cultural history he can recite without blinking. The way he moves through fights like he’s done it before.
He leaned back in his chair, eyes narrowing slightly.
What the hell was his life like before Japan?

Elsewhere, in two different places, the same search was underway.
In his apartment, Shinra Kishitani sat at his computer with the kind of bright, focused intensity he usually reserved for particularly interesting injuries. He had run the usual channels—school records, immigration traces, surface-level background. Almost nothing came back clean. Curtis Nightstar existed just enough to be enrolled at Raira and not enough to have a proper past.
Then the old missing-child poster loaded.
Northwest Territories. A boy with the same face structure, the same mismatched potential in the eyes, the same dark hair that would one day be braided. The age lined up. The resemblance was not close. It was exact.
Shinra went very still, the cheerful curiosity draining into something sharper and more unsettled.
“…Oh,” he murmured. “That’s not a coincidence.”
Across the city, Izaya Orihara stared at the same poster on a different screen, smile thinning into something colder and far more interested.
A missing child from the Northwest Territories who had grown into the foreigner currently walking around Ikebukuro cracking pavement and reading people like open books. No clean records. No easy trail. Just a gap where a childhood should have been and a young man who had arrived in Japan already dangerous.
Izaya’s fingers steepled in front of his mouth.
“How fascinating,” he whispered. “You didn’t just come here. You escaped something.”
He was still staring at the poster when the search returned nothing else of use.

At Shinra’s apartment, the door opened.
Celty stepped inside, yellow helmet turning toward the desk. She stopped when she saw the missing-child poster still open on the screen—the boy’s face unmistakable even at that age.
Her gloved hand hovered near her PDA.
Shinra didn’t look away from the image.
“Celty,” he said quietly. “I think our new friend has been missing for a very long time.”
Celty lifted her PDA and typed, the screen glowing in the dimmer light of the apartment.
He mentioned his parents abused him.
Do you think they abandoned him and covered it up?
Shinra finally looked away from the missing-child poster. The usual brightness in his expression had thinned into something more serious and unsettled.
“It’s possible,” he said slowly. “The abuse tracks with what he already told us—being hit for complaining, having to apologize for falling, the way he talks about near-death like it’s routine. If the parents wanted him gone, reporting him missing and then letting the trail die would be cleaner than admitting what they did.”
He turned the monitor slightly so Celty could see the details more clearly.
“But there’s a problem with the geography.” Shinra tapped the location listed on the poster. “This says he went missing in the Northwest Territories. Not Appalachia. He specifically told us he grew up in the Appalachian forest… and that his parents abandoned him there.”
A short, thoughtful silence.
“So either he was taken from the Territories and ended up in Appalachia, or the official record only captured the first disappearance and everything after that never made it onto paper.” Shinra’s eyes narrowed behind his glasses. “A child vanishes in the Northwest Territories. Years later a young man with the same face is walking around Ikebukuro with Rh-null blood, a body full of scars, and the ability to hear you. The gap between those two points is doing a lot of work.”
He leaned back in the chair, staring at the boy in the poster again.
“I don’t think this was a normal missing-child case, Celty. I think someone wanted him gone—and succeeded long enough for the world to stop looking.”
Across the city, Izaya Orihara spun once in his chair and came to a stop facing the monitor.
The missing-child poster from the Northwest Territories was still open. The boy’s face—same structure, same dark hair, the clear promise of the man currently walking around Ikebukuro—stared back at him. No useful follow-up records. No clean trail. Just a gap where a childhood should have been and a young man who had arrived in Japan already capable of putting Shizuo on the pavement.
Izaya’s smile widened, bright and delighted.
“Oh, I like this one,” he murmured. “Abused, abandoned, missing, and somehow still walking around with enough strength and control to make the city notice him. And almost no paper trail.”
He steepled his fingers, eyes gleaming.
“I want to know more.”
The information broker leaned forward and began opening new searches, already treating Curtis Nightstar as the most interesting new variable Ikebukuro had handed him in months.

Elsewhere, the knowledge remained carefully separated.
The van gang and Masaomi now knew Curtis could read color-gang history from body language and micro-expressions, that he was studying for detective work, and that he spoke Russian. They did not know about the white smoke, the helmeted night rides, or the full extent of his scars.
Rio knew the most: the apartment, the different outfits, the motorcycle, the quieter version of him that answered the door half-asleep and made tea. She still did not know about Saika, the missing poster, or what he had done in the alley with Anri.
Anri knew two separate people—the calm class representative with the braids who had taken her hand in the hallway, and the sealed, smoke-hidden stranger in the glowing helmet who had pulled her out of the fight with Celty and asked for nothing. She had no reason yet to believe they were the same man.
Celty and Shinra were only beginning to piece together the missing-child record and the abuse he had mentioned. They did not know what the van gang had learned over sushi, and the van gang did not know what was sitting on Shinra’s screen.
Izaya, for now, had the poster and his own hunger for more.
The city kept its pieces of Curtis Nightstar separated.
For the moment.